::STAT_U05_L07_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L07_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.7
Question:
A simulation shows differences from -2 to 2 are common, but the observed difference is 8. What does this suggest?

{
=A. The observed difference is unusual under the model#Correct. An observed difference far outside the simulated range may be statistically meaningful.
~B. The difference is always caused by bias#Bias is possible in some studies, but the simulation result itself points to unusual evidence.
~C. The model is automatically perfect#An unusual result challenges the model rather than proving it perfect.
~D. No conclusion can ever be made#The simulation gives evidence that can support a cautious conclusion.
}

::STAT_U05_L07_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L07_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.1
Question:
Which feature makes a statistical report more trustworthy?

{
~A. It uses a large headline only#A headline alone does not show statistical quality.
=B. It explains the sampling method and supports conclusions with data#Correct. A trustworthy report explains methods and connects claims to evidence.
~C. It hides the graph scale#Hiding scale can make a graph misleading.
~D. It claims causation from any association#Causation requires an appropriate design, usually a randomized experiment.
}

::STAT_U05_L07_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L07_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.7
Question:
What is practical significance?

{
~A. Whether all samples are identical#Samples vary; identical samples are not required.
~B. Whether a graph has bright colors#Visual design does not determine practical importance.
=C. Whether a difference is large enough to matter in context#Correct. Practical significance asks whether the size of the effect matters.
~D. Whether the sample size is zero#A sample size of zero gives no useful evidence.
}

::STAT_U05_L07_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L07_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.1
Question:
A report shows a graph with a cut-off vertical axis that exaggerates a small change. What should you question?

{
~A. Whether random assignment creates bias#Random assignment usually reduces bias in treatment comparisons.
~B. Whether all statistics are impossible#One misleading graph does not make all statistics impossible.
~C. Whether tables can ever be used#Tables can be useful when interpreted correctly.
=D. Whether the graph scale is misleading#Correct. A cut-off axis can make a small change look larger than it is.
}

